    "dependencies": [],
    "notes": "Cross-check implementation only; paper Falcon performance is from VecFalcon. This revision documents a 2026 best guess of future FIPS 206, not a published final standard. Compared with VecFalcon 61b326e: public keys are NTT encoded, encodings/hash-to-point are little-endian, signature infinity norm is capped at 840 instead of 2047, a 64-byte mu binds the hashed public key/context/message, secret keys include an additional 64-byte public-key hash, the base sampler uses 79 bits rather than 72, and SHAKE256x4 was removed. It is not a drop-in original round-3 Falcon implementation or interoperable wire format with the earlier VecFalcon API, despite the same public-key/signature lengths. Signatures remain padded 666/1280 bytes; exact unpadding parses the LSB-first compressed polynomial and checks against comp_decode. The scalar ref disables SIMD and uses software binary64, AVX2 uses native SIMD/FPU. Native degree-specific transcript KATs are mandatory for both. Public C API exposes only encoded keys; ExpandedSign is unavailable. Revision 2026-11-10: c-fn-dsa is primary and VecFalcon backup. Main ref now uses upstream SSE2 hardware binary64 with AVX2 disabled; it contains SSE2 intrinsics and is not a pure-C portable double path. The source has no FALCON_FPNATIVE switch. Software binary64 is supplemental only. GCC -O3 -march=native plus backend-required options; byte keys, 32-byte messages, fresh key/sign/verify triples, 20 warmup triples and unchanged Dilithium timing files. Every backend pair cross-verifies; equality and first differences are recorded separately. Original local FULL mismatch evidence remains pending until the original vectors are inspected. Current rule: -ffp-contract=off mandatory for all variants; runtime object/executable FMA audit must be zero. c-fn-dsa is primary, VecFalcon backup.",
